1
00:00:03,068 --> 00:00:05,329
Tonight, the sky is all orange.

2
00:00:05,473 --> 00:00:07,394
Yet this afternoon, it was blue!

3
00:00:09,358 --> 00:00:10,749
Mila has an idea:

4
00:00:10,839 --> 00:00:12,977
the Sun changes color in the evening!

5
00:00:14,356 --> 00:00:16,264
Curio looks at the Sun up close:

6
00:00:16,514 --> 00:00:17,974
it's still the same color.

7
00:00:18,093 --> 00:00:19,922
It's not the Sun that changes.

8
00:00:20,598 --> 00:00:21,989
Discovery mission!

9
00:00:25,612 --> 00:00:27,506
At noon, the Sun is high.

10
00:00:27,636 --> 00:00:29,284
Its light crosses only a thin

11
00:00:29,334 --> 00:00:31,527
layer of air before reaching you.

12
00:00:32,388 --> 00:00:34,445
In the evening, the Sun is low.

13
00:00:34,575 --> 00:00:35,544
Its light has to cross

14
00:00:35,594 --> 00:00:37,040
a huge amount of air.

15
00:00:37,171 --> 00:00:38,630
Much, much more.

16
00:00:40,168 --> 00:00:40,989
On the way,

17
00:00:41,146 --> 00:00:44,739
the little blue waves bounce off the air beads and scatter.

18
00:00:44,896 --> 00:00:46,315
They no longer reach you.

19
00:00:47,242 --> 00:00:48,333
The big red and orange

20
00:00:48,383 --> 00:00:50,277
waves go almost straight through.

21
00:00:50,421 --> 00:00:52,396
They are the ones that reach your eyes.

22
00:00:53,772 --> 00:00:56,277
That's why the sky turns orange in the evening.

23
00:00:57,772 --> 00:01:00,330
In the evening, blue gets scattered on the way:

24
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:01,622
orange is left.

25
00:01:02,507 --> 00:01:03,448
With an adult:

26
00:01:03,823 --> 00:01:06,560
look at the lamp through the glass of water and milk.

27
00:01:06,695 --> 00:01:09,166
It looks orange, like the evening Sun!

28
00:01:09,878 --> 00:01:12,016
And why does hair stand up with a balloon?

29
00:01:12,174 --> 00:01:13,552
The answer tomorrow.
